+1.3 An interior surgery disk with boundary on $S$ has boundary nullhomotopic in $M$, hence bounds a disk $E'\subset S$ by [F1]. Surgery splits off the sphere consisting of these two disks. It bounds a ball by hypothesis. That ball contains no other component of $S$, nor the punctured remainder of the same component. A different closed component inside it has a loop essential on that positive-genus component, contradicting [F1]'s injection into $M$; a different proper component cannot be inside an interior ball because its original boundary lies on $\partial M$. For the same component $C$, the connected remainder $\overline{C\setminus E'}$ has its interior on one side of the surgery sphere. If $C$ has original proper boundary, that boundary lies outside the interior ball, so the remainder is on the outside. If $C$ is closed, the punctured remainder has positive genus and contains a loop still essential in $C$: take a handle loop away from the removed disk, or move any essential loop of $C$ off that disk by replacing its finitely many disk-crossing arcs along the disk boundary. If the remainder were inside the ball, this loop would be nullhomotopic in $M$, again contradicting [F1]. Thus the punctured closed remainder is excluded by injectivity, not merely by having boundary. Consequently, after deleting the split-off sphere, the surgery is a disk replacement across an empty ball, hence an ambient isotopy. For a boundary surgery disk with inessential arc on $S$, that arc cuts off a disk $E'$; the union is a proper disk, so the disk-ball hypothesis gives the analogous empty boundary ball. A different component inside this boundary ball, even if its original proper boundary lies on the ball's boundary patch in $\partial M$, would have an essential loop nullhomotopic in $M$, contrary to [F1]; all components other than spheres and disks have such a loop. For the component undergoing the boundary surgery, removing the cutoff boundary disk $E'$ leaves a connected remainder whose inclusion into the original component induces an isomorphism on fundamental groups: restoring $E'$ attaches a disk along the single boundary interval given by the surgery arc. That remainder therefore contains a loop essential in the original nondisk component, and cannot be on the inside of the boundary ball. Its original boundary outside the ball, when present, also forces the outside side. No other surface can enter the ball through the patch in $\partial M$, because proper surface interiors lie in $\operatorname{int}M$ and the surgery disk is disjoint from the whole family. The required ball is therefore empty of the remainder of the surface family. Deleting the split-off boundary-parallel disk realizes this surgery by an isotopy preserving $\partial M$. To make the isotopies explicit in either case, add a narrow collar to the empty ball, prescribe the disk replacement on its two parametrized sides, and extend the matching disk maps over the side balls using [F2]. This gives a homeomorphism fixed on the free outer boundary of a ball or boundary half-ball. The Alexander interpolation $h_s(x)=s h(x/s)$ on the scaled convex ball and the identity outside it gives an isotopy to the identity; in a half-ball scale about a point on its flat boundary. It preserves that boundary and is PL on finite conical subdivisions. [F1, F2, F3, given, construct]
 
 2.1 If such a disk family in a tetrahedron has some disk boundary meeting an edge twice, traverse that edge through the cut balls. Its successive disk crossings give an edge walk in the dual tree from step 1.1. A walk without consecutive inverse edges cannot repeat a tree edge, since the unique reduced path between two vertices has no repeated edge. A repeated disk therefore forces a consecutive inverse pair somewhere in this walk. Thus two consecutive surface intersection points on this edge belong to the same disk $E$, on the same face of one cut ball. Let $\beta$ be the edge segment between them, and join its endpoints by an embedded proper arc $\alpha$ in that disk face. On the cut ball's boundary sphere $\alpha\cup\beta$ is a simple PL circle. A boundary disk that it bounds can be pushed into the ball relative to its boundary by a small collar. Its interior then misses every disk face. We obtain a PL bigon with boundary $\alpha\cup\beta$ and interior disjoint from the surface and the tetrahedron boundary. This constructs the returning-edge bigon rather than assuming the picture in a normal-surface diagram. [F1, F2, F3, step 1.1, construct]
 
